<h1>Understanding Mortgage Payments</h1>
<p class="article-lede">The Mortgage Calculator estimates the monthly cost of a fixed-rate, amortizing loan. Its headline figure is a total monthly payment — principal and interest, plus property tax, homeowner insurance, HOA dues and PMI whenever you enter them. This guide explains each part, starting with the principal-and-interest calculation at its core.</p>
<nav class="article-toc" aria-label="On this page"><p>On this page</p><ul><li><a href="#terms">The terms</a></li><li><a href="#example">Worked example</a></li><li><a href="#what-changes">What changes the result</a></li><li><a href="#not-included">What is not included</a></li><li><a href="#references-title">References</a></li></ul></nav>
<div class="article-body">
<h2 id="terms">The terms behind the estimate</h2><dl class="article-terms"><div><dt>Principal</dt><dd>The amount borrowed: home price minus down payment.</dd></div><div><dt>Down payment</dt><dd>The portion of the home price paid upfront in this model.</dd></div><div><dt>Interest rate</dt><dd>The entered annual percentage rate of interest, divided by 12 for the monthly calculation. Other loan costs are not modeled.</dd></div><div><dt>Loan term</dt><dd>The number of years over which the model repays the loan; years × 12 gives the monthly payment count.</dd></div><div><dt>Property tax</dt><dd>An annual amount you can enter. The calculator divides it by 12 and adds it to the monthly total.</dd></div><div><dt>Homeowner insurance</dt><dd>An annual amount you can enter, also divided by 12 and added to the monthly total.</dd></div><div><dt>HOA dues</dt><dd>A monthly amount you can enter, added to the monthly total as entered.</dd></div><div><dt>Private mortgage insurance (PMI)</dt><dd>An estimated monthly charge while the down payment is under 20% of the price: principal × the PMI rate you enter ÷ 12. In this model it stops once the loan balance reaches 80% of the home price.</dd></div></dl>
<h2 id="example">Worked example</h2><div class="article-example"><span class="example-label">Worked example</span><h3>From home price to monthly payment</h3><span class="example-label">Inputs</span><p>Use a $400,000 home price, an $80,000 down payment, 6.5% annual interest and a 30-year term.</p><span class="example-label">Calculation</span><ol><li><span>Principal: $400,000 − $80,000</span><strong data-example="loan-amount">$320,000.00</strong></li><li><span>Monthly rate: 6.5 ÷ 1,200</span><strong>0.00541667 (rounded)</strong></li><li><span>Payments: 30 × 12</span><strong>360 months</strong></li><li class="example-result"><span class="example-label">Result</span><span>Estimated monthly principal + interest</span><strong data-example="monthly-payment">$2,022.62</strong></li></ol><span class="example-label">Explanation</span><p class="example-caption">The calculation uses the unrounded monthly rate; displayed currency rounds to cents.</p></div>
<details class="article-formula"><summary>See the payment formula</summary><p><var>M</var> = <var>P</var> × <var>r</var> × (1 + <var>r</var>)<sup>n</sup> ÷ ((1 + <var>r</var>)<sup>n</sup> − 1).</p><p><var>P</var> is principal, <var>r</var> is the monthly rate and <var>n</var> is the number of payments. At 0% interest, the calculator uses <var>M</var> = <var>P</var> ÷ <var>n</var>. Total paid is <var>M</var> × <var>n</var>; total interest is that amount minus principal.</p></details>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Reading the result:</strong> this example enters no property tax, insurance, HOA dues or PMI, so the calculator's total monthly payment equals the principal-and-interest figure above. Enter those costs and the total rises by their monthly amounts — a principal-and-interest-only figure is not the full monthly cost of owning the home.</p></div>
<h2 id="what-changes">What changes the result?</h2><p>With other inputs unchanged, a larger down payment reduces principal and a higher interest rate increases the payment. At a positive fixed rate, a longer term lowers the monthly payment but increases total interest in this model.</p><p>The optional costs are added the same way every time: property tax and homeowner insurance as one-twelfth of the annual amounts you enter, HOA dues as the monthly amount you enter, and PMI while the down payment is under 20% of the price. Optional extra monthly payments and a one-time lump sum are also modeled — they shorten the payoff schedule and reduce total interest.</p>
<h2 id="not-included">What is not included?</h2><p>The estimate only covers the amounts you enter. It does not include closing costs or lender fees such as origination charges, and it does not model rate changes — the entered rate stays fixed for the whole term. It is an educational estimate, not a lender quote.</p>
